## THE QUESTIONS

1. **Is the repair CORRECT?** Check the ARM encodings by hand: `0xe3a00802` = `mov r0,#0x20000`?
   `0xe2800002` = `add r0,r0,#2`? `0xe8900002` = `ldmia r0,{r1}`? `0xe8800002` = `stmia r0,{r1}`?
   `0xe59f0008` = `ldr r0,[pc,#8]`? **A wrong encoding is invisible when the probe reads back the
   same wrong thing** — this project has a recorded gate row that measured the wrong register for
   months.
2. **Does `0x77881122` follow?** Under `& ~1` a base of `0x20002` reads bytes `[2..5]` =
   `22 11 88 77`; little-endian that is `0x77881122`. **Derive it yourself rather than accepting
   mine**, and do the same for the STM `0x33440000`.
3. *** THE FLOOR QUESTION, ASKED OF THE SHIPPED COMPOSITE, NOT JUST THIS DIFF: does a clean-sweep
   mutant still exist in the clause-(d) enumeration? *** The enumeration is the predicates
   DOMINATING the edited lines, walked to the enclosing function boundary, plus operand swaps drawn
   from fields the guarded body reads. **The fix is `addr & 0xfffffffc` at two `memory_rw` call
   sites. Name any weakening that all 29 rows would now miss** — `& ~7`? a conditional mask? a mask
   applied at one site and a different one at the other? **This project has a measured case where a
   repair closed the gap at the address the mutant used and left the symmetric cell open.**
4. **Is `+3` correctly NOT rowed?** My reasoning: `~1` dies at `+2`, `~2` already dies at `+1`, so a
   `+3` row kills no enumerated mutant and rows are owed per enumerated mutant. **Is there a mutant
   that `+3` kills and neither `+1` nor `+2` does?** If yes, the disclosure is wrong and a row is
   owed.
5. **Are the two new PATH controls doing real work**, or are they decoration? They assert the `+2`
   runs took the COLD path. **Under which mutants can that channel vary?** If none, say so — a row
   whose channel cannot vary under any enumerated mutant is vacuous by construction.
6. **`M_MASK1` fails exactly 2 of 29. Is that tight or thin?** Two rows is the minimum that closes
   the enumerated mutant. **Is there a plausible variant of `M_MASK1` — same shape, different
   weakening — that the two new rows would MISS?**
7. **DENOMINATOR FOR YOUR ZERO.** If you find no floor breach, say **which cells and which mutant
   shapes you swept** to reach that. A bare zero does not close the chain; a zero with an
   enumeration does.

