7855: A4.1.20 LDM (1)
7856: 
7857:        31         28 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 19     16 15                                                      0
7858: 
7859:            cond 1 0 0 P U 0 W 1 Rn                         register_list
7860: 
7861:        LDM (1) (Load Multiple) loads a non-empty subset, or possibly all, of the general-purpose registers from
7862:        sequential memory locations. It is useful for block loads, stack operations and procedure exit sequences.
7863: 
7864:        The general-purpose registers loaded can include the PC. If they do, the word loaded for the PC is treated
7865:        as an address and a branch occurs to that address. In ARMv5 and above, bit[0] of the loaded value
7866:        determines whether execution continues after this branch in ARM state or in Thumb state, as though a BX
7867:        (loaded_value) instruction had been executed (but see also The T and J bits on page A2-15 for operation on
7868:        non-T variants of ARMv5). In earlier versions of the architecture, bits[1:0] of the loaded value are ignored
7869:        and execution continues in ARM state, as though the instruction MOV PC,(loaded_value) had been executed.
7870: 
7871:        Syntax
7872: 
7873:        LDM{<cond>}<addressing_mode> <Rn>{!}, <registers>
7874: 
7875:        where:
7876: 
7877:        <cond>       Is the condition under which the instruction is executed. The conditions are defined in The
7878:                     condition field on page A3-3. If <cond> is omitted, the AL (always) condition is used.
7879: 
7880:        <addressing_mode>
7881: 
7882:                           Is described in Addressing Mode 4 - Load and Store Multiple on page A5-41. It determines
7883:                           the P, U, and W bits of the instruction.
7884: 
7885:        <Rn>         Specifies the base register used by <addressing_mode>. Using R15 as the base register <Rn>
7886: 
7887:                     gives an UNPREDICTABLE result.
7888: 
7889:        !            Sets the W bit, causing the instruction to write a modified value back to its base register Rn
7890: 
7891:                     as specified in Addressing Mode 4 - Load and Store Multiple on page A5-41. If ! is omitted,
7892: 
7893:                     the W bit is 0 and the instruction does not change its base register in this way. (However, if
7894: 
7895:                     the base register is included in <registers>, it changes when a value is loaded into it.)
7896: 
7897:        <registers>
7898: 
7899:                     Is a list of registers, separated by commas and surrounded by { and }. It specifies the set of
7900:                     registers to be loaded by the LDM instruction.
7901: 
7902:                     The registers are loaded in sequence, the lowest-numbered register from the lowest memory
7903:                     address (start_address), through to the highest-numbered register from the highest memory
7904:                     address (end_address). If the PC is specified in the register list (opcode bit[15] is set),
7905:                     the instruction causes a branch to the address (data) loaded into the PC.
7906: 
7907:                     For each of i=0 to 15, bit[i] in the register_list field of the instruction is 1 if Ri is in the list
7908:                     and 0 otherwise. If bits[15:0] are all zero, the result is UNPREDICTABLE.
7909: 
7910: A4-36             Copyright c 1996-1998, 2000, 2004, 2005 ARM Limited. All rights reserved.  ARM DDI 0100I
7911:                                                                                           ARM Instructions
7912: 
7913: Architecture version
7914: All.
7915: 
7916: Exceptions
7917: Data Abort.
7918: 
7919: Operation
7920: 
7921: MemoryAccess(B-bit, E-bit)
7922: if ConditionPassed(cond) then
7923: 
7924:       address = start_address
7925: 
7926:       for i = 0 to 14
7927:             if register_list[i] == 1 then
7928:                   Ri = Memory[address,4]
7929:                   address = address + 4
7930: 
7931:       if register_list[15] == 1 then
7932:             value = Memory[address,4]
7933:             if (architecture version 5 or above) then
7934:                   pc = value AND 0xFFFFFFFE
7935:                   T Bit = value[0]
7936:             else
7937:                   pc = value AND 0xFFFFFFFC
7938:             address = address + 4
7939: 
7940:       assert end_address == address - 4
7941: 
7942: Notes
7943: 
7944: Operand restrictions
7945: 
7946:                If the base register <Rn> is specified in <registers>, and base register write-back is specified,
7947:                the final value of <Rn> is UNPREDICTABLE.
7948: 
7949: Data Abort For details of the effects of the instruction if a Data Abort occurs, see Effects of data-aborted
7950:                    instructions on page A2-21.
7951: 
7952: Non word-aligned addresses
7953: 
7954:                For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0, the Load Multiple instructions ignore the least significant two
7955:                bits of the address. If an implementation includes a System Control coprocessor
7956:                (see Chapter B3 The System Control Coprocessor), an address with bits[1:0] != 0b00 causes
7957:                an alignment exception if alignment checking is enabled.
7958: 
7959:                For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 1, all non-word aligned accesses cause an alignment fault.
7960: 
