14019: A4.1.97 STM (1)
14020: 
14021: 31               28 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 19  16 15                                                     0
14022: 
14023:                cond 1 0 0 P U 0 W 0 Rn                 register_list
14024: 
14025: STM (1) (Store Multiple) stores a non-empty subset (or possibly all) of the general-purpose registers to
14026: sequential memory locations.
14027: 
14028: Syntax
14029: 
14030: STM{<cond>}<addressing_mode> <Rn>{!}, <registers>
14031: 
14032: where:
14033: 
14034: <cond>             Is the condition under which the instruction is executed. The conditions are defined
14035:                    in The condition field on page A3-3. If <cond> is omitted, the AL (always) condition
14036:                    is used.
14037: 
14038: <addressing_mode>
14039: 
14040:                    Is described in Addressing Mode 4 - Load and Store Multiple on page A5-41. It
14041:                    determines the P, U, and W bits of the instruction.
14042: 
14043: <Rn>               Specifies the base register used by <addressing_mode>. If R15 is specified as <Rn>,
14044: 
14045:                    the result is UNPREDICTABLE.
14046: 
14047: !                  Sets the W bit, causing the instruction to write a modified value back to its base
14048: 
14049:                    register Rn as specified in Addressing Mode 4 - Load and Store Multiple on
14050: 
14051:                    page A5-41. If ! is omitted, the W bit is 0 and the instruction does not change its
14052: 
14053:                    base register in this way.
14054: 
14055: <registers>        Is a list of registers, separated by commas and surrounded by { and }. It specifies the
14056:                    set of registers to be stored by the STM instruction.
14057: 
14058:                    The registers are stored in sequence, the lowest-numbered register to the lowest
14059:                    memory address (start_address), through to the highest-numbered register to the
14060:                    highest memory address (end_address).
14061: 
14062:                    For each of i=0 to 15, bit[i] in the register_list field of the instruction is 1 if Ri is in
14063:                    the list and 0 otherwise. If bits[15:0] are all zero, the result is UNPREDICTABLE.
14064: 
14065:                    If R15 is specified in <registers>, the value stored is IMPLEMENTATION DEFINED.
14066:                    For more details, see Reading the program counter on page A2-9.
14067: 
14068: Architecture version
14069: All.
14070: 
14071: ARM DDI 0100I  Copyright c 1996-1998, 2000, 2004, 2005 ARM Limited. All rights reserved.       A4-189
14072: ARM Instructions
14073: 
14074:         Exceptions
14075:         Data Abort.
14076: 
14077:         Operation
14078: 
14079:         MemoryAccess(B-bit, E-bit)
14080:         processor_id = ExecutingProcessor()
14081:         if ConditionPassed(cond) then
14082: 
14083:               address = start_address
14084:               for i = 0 to 15
14085: 
14086:                     if register_list[i] == 1 then
14087:                           Memory[address,4] = Ri
14088:                           address = address + 4
14089:                           if Shared(address) then /* from ARMv6 */
14090:                                 physical_address = TLB(address)
14091:                                 ClearExclusiveByAddress(physical_address,processor_id,4)
14092:                                 /* See Summary of operation on page A2-49 */
14093: 
14094:               assert end_address == address - 4
14095: 
14096:         Usage
14097: 
14098:         STM is useful as a block store instruction (combined with LDM it allows efficient block copy) and for stack
14099:         operations. A single STM used in the sequence of a procedure can push the return address and general-purpose
14100:         register values on to the stack, updating the stack pointer in the process.
14101: 
14102:         Notes
14103: 
14104:         Operand restrictions
14105:                            If <Rn> is specified in <registers> and base register write-back is specified:
14106:                            � If <Rn> is the lowest-numbered register specified in <registers>, the original value of
14107:                                    <Rn> is stored.
14108:                            � Otherwise, the stored value of <Rn> is UNPREDICTABLE.
14109: 
14110:         Data Abort For details of the effects of the instruction if a Data Abort occurs, see Effects of data-aborted
14111:                            instructions on page A2-21.
14112: 
14113:         Non word-aligned addresses
14114: 
14115:                            For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0, the STM[1] instruction ignores the least significant two bits of
14116:                            address. For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 1, all non-word aligned accesses cause an alignment fault.
14117: 
14118:         Alignment    If an implementation includes a System Control coprocessor (see Chapter B3 The System
14119:                      Control Coprocessor), and alignment checking is enabled, an address with bits[1:0] != 0b00
14120:                      causes an alignment exception.
14121: 
14122:         Time order   The time order of the accesses to individual words of memory generated by this instruction
14123:                      is only defined in some circumstances. See Memory access restrictions on page B2-13 for
14124:                      details.
14125: 
