18661: A5.2.2 Load and Store Word or Unsigned Byte - Immediate offset
18662: 
18663:        31      28 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 19  16 15      12 11                                       0
18664: 
18665:            cond 0 1 0 1 U B 0 L Rn                   Rd                    offset_12
18666: 
18667:        This addressing mode calculates an address by adding or subtracting the value of an immediate offset to or
18668:        from the value of the base register Rn.
18669: 
18670:        Syntax
18671: 
18672:        [<Rn>, #+/-<offset_12>]
18673: 
18674:        where:
18675: 
18676:        <Rn>           Specifies the register containing the base address.
18677: 
18678:        <offset_12>    Specifies the immediate offset used with the value of Rn to form the address.
18679: 
18680:        Operation
18681: 
18682:        if U == 1 then
18683:              address = Rn + offset_12
18684: 
18685:        else /* U == 0 */
18686:              address = Rn - offset_12
18687: 
18688:        Usage
18689: 
18690:        This addressing mode is useful for accessing structure (record) fields, and accessing parameters and local
18691:        variables in a stack frame. With an offset of zero, the address produced is the unaltered value of the base
18692:        register Rn.
18693: 
18694:        Notes
18695:        Offset of zero The syntax [<Rn>] is treated as an abbreviation for [<Rn>, #0], unless the instruction is one
18696: 
18697:                           that only allows post-indexed addressing modes (LDRBT, LDRT, STRBT or STRT).
18698:        The B bit This bit distinguishes between an unsigned byte (B==1) and a word (B==0) access.
18699:        The L bit This bit distinguishes between a Load (L==1) and a Store (L==0) instruction.
18700:        Use of R15 If R15 is specified as register Rn, the value used is the address of the instruction plus eight.
